WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.040
I’m often asked
what my opinion

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
about a diet
or disease is.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.920
Who cares what my or
anyone else’s opinion is.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.000
All we should care about
is what the science says.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.160
What does the best available
balance of evidence

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.080
Published in the peer-
reviewed medical literature

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.360
have to say right now?

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.800
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.360
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.040
It’s time for the Nutrition
Facts Grab Bag,

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.960
where we look at
the latest science

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.800
on a whole variety
of topics.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.000
And we start today with
a story on the micromort.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.400
And no, that’s not a nickname
for your short uncle.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.400
Think of it as a unit of comparing
and communicating risk to patients.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.880
Hundreds of millions of operations
are performed every year,

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.680
and the risk of death is
typically around a half a percent,

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.560
to which patients might say things like,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.360
“I could die just as
easily crossing the road,”

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:06.960
making it clear they
really don’t understand

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.000
the difference in magnitude of risk.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.640
One way to communicate
risk is by analogy.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.400
For example, just going
under anesthetic carries about

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.840
a 1 in 100,000 chance you won’t
wake up. How much is that?

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.760
Well, that’s about the same
risk as an expert sky dive.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.440
Okay, but that still may be kind
of hard to wrap your head around.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.440
It's hard to think in
terms of small numbers.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:43.440
Imagine discussing a
0.0017 mile x 0.00227 mile rug.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.360
How big even is that?
We need more digestible units.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.640
Enter the micromort
as a unit of comparing

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.360
and communicating risk to patients.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:03.120
A micromort is a unit equivalent to
one in a million chance of dying.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.680
One in a million is like
the chances of ﬂipping a coin

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.080
and getting 20 heads or tails in a row,

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.160
or a little less than the
chances of getting a royal flush.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:20.080
But the real utility is to help
compare different risks to one another,

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.240
using the same metric.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.280
For example, driving a
hundred miles entails about

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.880
a one in a million chance of
death, so that’s one micromort.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.960
Scuba diving is like
five micromorts per dive;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.640
so, each dive is as risky
as driving 500 miles.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.280
So, now we have a way to
directly compare the risk

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.160
of surgical procedures
and common activities.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.840
Giving birth is as risky as driving
from NY to LA and then back again,

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.960
but getting a cesarean is
more than twice as risky.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.200
Even something like a simple hernia
repair carries the same risk

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.560
of dying as like sky diving 200 times.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.240
Now obviously, sometimes
you have no choice,

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.400
but death from varicose vein
surgery or circumcision

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.320
could probably be avoided.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.400
I was surprised to learn
that regular horseback riding

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.200
is like four times deadlier
than rock climbing,

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.120
but getting chemo and radiation
for head and neck cancer

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.560
is riskier than rock-
climbing for 500 years,

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:33.200
driving 5 million miles, or
jumping 5,000 sky dives, etc.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.480
One leading cause of death
I really didn’t talk about

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.200
in How Not to Die is
accidental death.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.400
We have approximately a one
in a million chance of dying

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.520
just by accident every day of our lives,

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.960
and about half of that risk
is dying in a car crash,

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.400
based on U.S. averages.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.800
Then, there are all sorts of other ways.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.520
I am surprised to learn Americans
have about a 1 in 200,000 chance

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.240
every year of dying from a
foreign body entering an orifice

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:03.840
other than the mouth.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.640
Other things we may want to avoid
include climbing Mount Everest,

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.000
about 30 times riskier than
coal mining or base jumping.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:16.640
Trains and planes are actually
equivalent over the same distance,

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.280
but riding a motorcycle is about 50 times
deadlier than riding in a car,

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:25.200
though cycling to a
destination is riskier too,

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.240
about 10 times as deadly
as driving in the near term.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.560
Here’s a good example of how one
can use micromort comparisons

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.400
to help put things in perspective.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.360
Certain types of breast implants
can cause a rare type of cancer,

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.560
a type of breast implant-
associated lymphoma.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.360
You can imagine how scary this is

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.600
for the millions of women
who have implants,

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.400
but check out the risk compared to
the risk of other common activities.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.680
Your risk of dying from
this kind of cancer is less

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.160
than a single day of skiing.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.080
Now, it’s probably better to
die quick on the slopes

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:04.080
than all the slow suffering of cancer,

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:06.160
and risk of bankrupting your family,

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.960
but at least it can put the
risk of the implant-cancer

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.631
killing you in context.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:16.400
In our next story we ask,
do nut eaters live longer

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.120
simply because they swap
in protein from plants

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:20.800
in place of animal protein?

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.760
The American Journal of Clinical
Nutrition is the highest ranked

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:28.000
peer-reviewed scientific
journal in nutrition.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.520
That should tell you a lot about
the field, since it’s published

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:37.200
by the American Society of Nutrition,
whose sustaining partners

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:43.360
include The Sugar Association, candy
bar and soda companies,

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:48.800
the corn syrup people, and the meat,
dairy, and egg industries.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.840
And this is the highest
ranked nutrition journal.

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:56.320
The fact that the National Cattlemen’s
Beef Association is a

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:01.280
sustaining partner may help explain
their publication of this article.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:06.160
Imagine you’re in the pocket
of Big Beef and Big Pig.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:11.280
How could you possibly pull off a
study showing that eating red meat

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.440
does not negatively inﬂuence
cardiovascular disease risk factors?

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.200
A meta-analysis of randomized
controlled trials showing that

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:24.000
eating more versus less red
meat does not inﬂuence

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.680
cholesterol or blood pressure.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.080
Dr.’s Barnard and Willett pointed
out the fatal flaw in their editorial

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:35.760
“The Misuse of Meta-analysis
in Nutrition Research,”

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.440
by asking the question “Compared to What?”

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.160
Of the 39 trials that they
had chosen on LDL cholesterol,

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:48.480
nearly 90 percent of them were
just swapping one meat for another,

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:51.120
comparing red meat to white meat.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:58.400
They were using chicken control
diets or fish control diets.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.400
And we know that when it
comes to cholesterol,

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.640
the impact of beef consumption
is just as bad as fish or poultry,

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:06.560
and so that’s how they pulled it off.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:08.240
They just swapped meats.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:12.960
That’s like publishing a study saying
total Twinkie intake does not

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:18.080
negatively inﬂuence risk factors by
switching Twinkies with Ding Dongs.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.440
Those randomized to zero
Twinkies didn’t do any better.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.040
Yeah, because now they
were eating Ding Dongs.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:28.800
It’s a classic drug industry trick:

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.760
testing your drug against
something known to be terrible.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:37.200
Whereas if you swap out meat for
plant-based meats—plant-based sausages,

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.600
plant-based chicken
patties, and veggie dogs—

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:42.720
you end up with significantly
lower cholesterol.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:47.600
Well duh, you say, there’s less
saturated fat in plant-based meats.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:54.400
But even independent of saturated
fat content, you end up with

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.280
higher LDL cholesterol with
red meat or white meat—

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:01.440
any kind of meat—compared to
non-meat protein sources.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:04.480
The researchers conclude that
this is keeping with recommendations

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.840
promoting diets with a high
proportion of plant-based food but,

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:11.120
based on cholesterol effects, white
meat like chicken and turkey

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.440
is just as bad as red.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:22.240
And fish may be even worse, though
what they often did is try to

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:26.000
standardize the saturated fat
content by adding like butter.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:30.560
But at the same saturated fat
content, fish appears to be worse

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:35.360
than beef, and chicken is
just as bad as beef.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:39.520
Yet plant protein sources like
legumes, soy, and nuts–

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:43.680
so beans, split peas, chickpeas, lentils,
soybeans, and nuts—did better.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:48.880
Switch out a single serving of even
lean beef with the same amount

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:52.640
of calories of nuts or soybeans, and
you can lower LDL bad cholesterol,

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:55.840
a key risk factor for the
#1 killer of men and women

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:58.320
in the United States and
around much of the world.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:03.600
But wait, is that why a single
serving of nuts a day is associated

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.200
with 22 percent reduction in
the risk of premature death?

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:11.120
Why millions of deaths every
year may be attributable to

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:12.480
inadequate nut intake?

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.120
Maybe the benefit is just because
they’re eating nuts instead of meat?

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:22.400
No. The drop of heart attacks
amongst more frequent nut eaters

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:28.240
is just as strong or even
stronger among vegetarians.

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:31.120
The reduced mortality associated
with nut consumption

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:33.200
is independent of health condition.

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:36.080
It’s not just health nuts eating nuts.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:39.360
In fact, in a comparison of
a dozen different food groups,

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.040
nuts beat out even
vegetables when it came to

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.531
a lower risk of premature death.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:50.720
Finally today, we look at the secret
to the public health community’s triumph

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:54.725
when past attempts to regulate
the food industry had failed.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:59.600
There are three broad approaches to
mediating the ruin of risky choices:

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:01.920
inform people (like using labeling),

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:05.280
nudge people (for example, by
offering financial incentives),

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:08.080
or directly intervene to make
the activity less harmful.

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:11.280
Which do you think
prevented more car fatalities:

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:15.200
mandating driver education,
labeling cars about crash risk,

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:19.280
or removing the human element
altogether by just making sure

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:20.640
air bags are installed?

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:25.600
There are public education nutrition
campaigns from "sugar pack" ads

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:29.280
on public transit, informing consumers
how much sugar is in soft drinks,

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:33.600
to "Hot Dogs Cause Butt Cancer"
billboards, educating about the link

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:35.520
between processed meat
and colorectal cancer.

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:40.080
But is there a way to make products
nutritionally safer in the first place?

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:44.000
The ban on trans fats
offers a useful lesson.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:48.000
In 1993, the Harvard Nurses Study
found that high intake of trans fat

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:50.640
may increase the risk
of heart disease by 50%.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:54.480
That's where the trans fat
story started in Denmark,

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:55.760
and it ended a decade later

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:59.440
with a ban on added
trans fats there in 2003.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:02.880
It took another 10 years, though,
before the U.S. even started

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:07.440
considering a ban. All the while,
trans fats were killing an estimated

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:09.680
tens of thousands
of Americans every year,

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:13.040
resulting in as many years
of healthy life lost as conditions

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:16.640
like meningitis, cervical cancer,
and multiple sclerosis.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:19.760
If so many people were
suffering and dying,

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:23.680
why did it take so long for the U.S.
to even suggest taking action?

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:28.400
One can look at a fight over
New York City's trans fat ban

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:30.640
for a microcosm
of the national debate.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:34.720
Opposition came down hard from
the food industry, complaining

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:39.440
about government intrusion,
likening the city to a "nanny state."

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.560
Since trans fats can be naturally
found in meat and dairy,

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:45.920
the livestock industry echoed
the Institute of Shortening's

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:48.240
"everything-in-moderation" argument.

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:53.680
Critics styled such proposals
as the rise of food fascism.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:58.240
But it was the restaurant and food
industries that limited consumer choice

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:02.400
by so broadly fouling the food supply
with these dangerous fats.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:06.960
If food zealots get their wish
in banning added trans fats—

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:08.960
another argument went—
what's next?

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:13.440
Vested corporate interests tend to rally
around these kinds of slippery slope

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:18.640
arguments to try to distract from the
very real fact that people are dying.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.440
I mean what if the government
tries to make us eat broccoli?

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:26.720
This actually came up in a Supreme
Court case over Obamacare.

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:30.000
Chief Justice Roberts suggested
Congress could start

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.480
ordering everyone
to buy vegetables,

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:36.480
a fear Justice Ginsburg
dubbed the broccoli horrible.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:40.320
Technically, Congress could compel the
American public to eat more plant-based,

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:44.080
Justice Ginsburg wrote, yet
one can't offer the hypothetical

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:48.560
and unreal possibility of a vegetarian
state as a credible argument.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.640
As one legal scholar put it, "Judges
and lawyers live on the slippery slope

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:56.160
of analogies; they're not
supposed to ski it to the bottom."

00:12:57.040 --> 00:12:59.600
New York City finally
won its trans fat fight,

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:01.680
preserving its status
as a public health leader.

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:06.160
For example, New York banned lead
paint 18 years before federal action

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:10.160
was taken, despite decades of
unequivocal evidence of its harm.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:14.080
Comparing stroke and heart attack
rates before and after the rollout of

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:17.840
the trans fat ban in different New York
counties, researchers estimate it

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:20.960
successfully reduced cardiovascular
death rates by about 5%.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:25.440
This then became the model for
the nationwide ban years later.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.040
How was the public health
community able to triumph

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.840
when attempts in the past to regulate
the food industry failed?

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:35.520
If you would have asked me about
the odds of a national trans fat ban,

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:37.920
I would have said, "fat chance."

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:41.280
In Denmark, as a leading
Danish cardiologist put it,

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:44.160
"Instead of warning consumers
about trans fats and telling them

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:46.480
what they are, we've
simply removed them."

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:49.600
But we're Americans!
As they say here:

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:52.640
"You can put poison in the
food if you label it properly."

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:54.640
If people know the risks,
the argument goes,

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:56.640
then they should be able
to eat whatever they want.

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:59.360
But that's assuming they've
been given all the facts,

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:00.960
which isn't always the case,

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:04.560
given the food industry's model
of systemic dishonesty,

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:07.120
as one health ethics professor put it.

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:11.120
Given the predilection for predatory
deception and manipulation,

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:13.120
government intervention
was deemed necessary,

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:16.000
but how are you going
to get it passed?

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:18.240
First, there was a
labeling requirement.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:21.760
Manufacturers had to start adding
trans fat content to products'

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:26.080
nutrition facts labeling. Ostensibly
this was to influence consumers,

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:28.560
but it may have had a bigger
impact on producers.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:32.720
Now that they had to divulge
the truth, companies scrambled

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:36.880
to reformulate their products to gain
a "no trans fat" competitive edge.

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:41.040
Within years of the mandatory
disclosure, more than 5,000 products

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.720
were introduced touting low
or zero trans fats on their labels.

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:47.920
Kentucky Fried Chicken went from being
sued for having some of the highest

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:51.520
trans fat levels to running an ad
campaign where mom tells dad

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:54.800
in front of kids that KFC now
has zero grams of trans fat,

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:57.840
and the father yells,
"Yeah, baby! Whoooo!!"

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:00.640
and begins eating fried
chicken by the bucketful.

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:04.080
That was the secret
to passing the ban.

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:07.760
Once the major food industry players
had already reformulated their products

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:11.280
and bragged about it, once there
wasn't so much money at stake,

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:15.040
then there was insufficient
political will to block the ban

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:19.478
and added trans fats were
taken off the playing field.

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:22.800
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:26.080
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:28.640
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:31.840
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:36.080
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:39.360
please go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:41.960
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:46.080
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite to each of these topics.

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:50.240
For a timely text on the
pathogens that cause pandemics,

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:53.120
order the e-book,
audiobook, or hard copy

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:56.000
of my last book,
“How to Survive a Pandemic”.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:00.160
For recipes, check out
my second-to-last book,

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.400
my “How Not to Diet Cookbook”.

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:05.680
It’s beautifully designed
with more than 100 recipes

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:07.840
for delicious and
nutritious meals.

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.400
And all the proceeds
I receive from the sales

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:12.800
of all my books goes to charity.

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:16.960
NutritionFacts.org is a nonprofit,
science-based public service,

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:19.120
where you can sign up
for free daily updates

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:23.360
on the latest in nutrition research
via bite-sized videos and articles.

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:26.400
Everything on the
website is free.

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:28.720
There’s no ads, no
corporate sponsorship.

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:31.840
It’s strictly non-commercial.
I’m not selling anything.

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:35.280
I just put it up as a public service,
as a labor of love,

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:38.080
as a tribute to my grandmother,

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:49.840
whose own life was saved
with evidence-based nutrition.

